Z||000003||ChadMan||08-26-99||08:04 AM||||This brings me to an interesting question.  Do you think interest in the Fiero is rising, declining, or holding steady?
Z||000004||FieroGT||08-26-99||08:29 AM||||I think that we're getting to the point where the ratty Fieros are dying. I used to see many of them, now all I see are nice ones, and far and few between. I've noticed that a local junkyard that used to have 20 or so Fieros at a time now only has about three of them and they are exactly that: Junk. No good parts left at all. Remember a few years back when you saw 2nd gen Camaros ('70-'81) that were ratty and all over the place? Seems like there was one sitting in every other yard. How many have you seen lately? Probably not many, and the ones you have seen are nice. <p>     I'd say that were close to that point now. If anyone has the opportunity to get a parts car for cheap, do it. 5 years from now you may be very sorry. <br>
Z||000005||RFMike||08-26-99||09:29 PM||||Have to disagree with you about the number remaining, at least where I live.  I think there are more now than then.  A lot of the cars being bought are being rebuilt and restored.  <br>I do agree that there are less in the junk yards.  I have a friend that has been in the auto body business for 25 years, and that's what he tells me.  He gets parts from all over the country, so he has a handle on it.  
Z||000006||JimiZ||08-27-99||12:18 AM||||I'll agree.  Seems like I haven't gotten too many lines on Fieros lately for my salvage yard.  I recieved a line on an old 84 2m4 but the guy decided not to sell it.  Also found one in a farmer's field, but I can't find out who owns the property or the car so I can get it.  As far as the Camaros go, they're getting scarce here in the midwest too.  I guess the salt on the roads in the winter does them in.  350 Chevy engines are getting hard too.  I do have two nice 400 small block  Chevy engines I want to sell, and a nice built 454.  Also have some 4bolt main shortblock 350's for rebuilding if anybody interested.  I'm near St.Louis MO.  Why is it when they make something cool, or modifiable, and it creates a niche, they suddenly take it away?  First leaded gas, then Big Blocks, now <br>rear wheel drive and V8's, next all we'll have is Electric Schmuckmobiles.<br>Viva Internal Combustion, Shade Tree Mechanics and Aftermarket Tuner Parts!!!!<br>
Z||000007||DJRice||08-27-99||07:20 AM||||Here Here, Long Live Gas Guzzling 8 cylinder engines.  I have no intentions of ever getting rid of my 84 Buick Lesabre for that exact reason.  Nowadays, if you want RWD and a longitudinal mount V8, you have to buy a truck, but then, how long will that last?<p>
